Maria: Hey team — running into flaky logs in the onboarding sampleapp. Sometimes we get DEBUG lines with request-id, sometimes only INFO without request-id. It feels tied to OpenTelemetry hook ordering during SDK init. Anyone seen this?
Alex: Can you share the init sequence and the env vars you set? is this Node or Python sample?
Maria: Node sample. Start sequence is: install SDK -> require('redwood-sdk') -> configure logger -> init otel hooks -> start server. Env: LOG_LEVEL=info, REDWOOD_OTEL=1. Repro steps: run `npm start` and hit /health a few times. Attaching a minimal log snippet.
Maria: ```
INFO Server starting
DEBUG otel:hook:init registering hooks
INFO request handled route=/health
DEBUG request-id=abcd1234 trace=00-...
INFO request handled route=/metrics
```
Alex: Looks like the DEBUG lines are emitted after some requests already logged at INFO. Probably the logger autoconfig is happening lazily after the first OTEL callback. Might be a race where the req-id middleware isn't wired before the logger instance is used.
Sam: I hit the same when onboarding a new customer earlier. Workaround was to force-sync logger init before hooking OTEL: call SDK.initLogger(...) as the very first user call. Also bumping the SDK to 1.3.2 fixed a separate ordering bug.
Maria: Is there a small patch we can recommend to the sampleapp so new users don't hit this?
Alex: Two quick changes: 1) ensure the request-id middleware runs before route handlers and before any async OTEL exporter starts; 2) add an explicit early logger bootstrap in the sample to lock level. Example middleware (Node/Express):
Alex: ```js
// early.js - require this at top of main entry before anything else
const {initLogger, injectRequestId} = require('redwood-sdk');
initLogger({level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info'});
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  // simple req id fallback for first-log guarantees
  req.id = req.headers['x-request-id'] || generateShortId();
  process.env.CURRENT_REQUEST_ID = req.id; // used by logger integration
  next();
});
module.exports = app;
```
Sam: Also important: the SDK's OTEL hook attaches an async exporter that can flip the effective log level for a short window when it registers. We added a guard in 1.3.x that keeps the logger level stable until exporter is ready. Recommend sampleapp use SDK >=1.3.2 and include this early.js pattern.
Maria: That helps. What about PII scrubbing? We saw request headers occasionally leaking user emails in DEBUG logs.
Alex: There's a regex mismatch in the sample redaction rule: it assumes emails always have quotes. The current scrub rule is /(\"?\w+@\w+\.\w+\"?)/g which misses hyphens and dots in local-part. Use a more robust pattern or use the SDK's redactHeaders option.
Alex: Suggested replace: /(?:[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+)@(?:[A-Za-z0-9.-]+)\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g
Maria: Sweet. I'll patch the sample and add a short paragraph in onboarding doc: "Bootstrap logger before OTEL hooks, include request-id middleware, and prefer SDK >=1.3.2 for stable log levels."
Sam: @Maria also add a tiny integration test that starts the sample with LOG_LEVEL=info and asserts no DEBUG lines before first request-id assigned. Saved us a bug previously. :thumbsup:
devex-bot: Note: created issue #4822 in devex/samples to track: "sampleapp: early logger bootstrap + request-id guard + improved redaction regex"
Maria: Thanks — I'll send a PR later today with the init change + regex + test.
Alex: Ping me on the PR and I'll review.
Maria: Will do. Cheers!
